tively I actually tried this  routine for a couple of weeks because  what I don't think that requires an  explanation it's fairly taxing and  definitely good for mobility  I felt pretty beat up even though I was  doing it with a very light barbell at  first I will say though that I  personally would add in some kind of  triceps training especially if you're  using the first routine there's an  isolated biceps move so why not triceps  there's also an awful lot of laps in  there so it kind of makes sense to me  to swap out the pullovers for skull  crushers and personally I'd leave out  the good mornings to and maybe stick  some deadlifts in there instead but what  can we take from this well the first  thing that's interesting to note is that  is training involved into a full body  routine consisting of many more compound  movements with the exception of curls  this meant that each muscle group was  being hit three times a week which could  have potentially increases protein  synthesis there was only very moderate  intensity it's also noticeable for its  simplicity and it could actually easily  pass for quite a modern training program  bruseles stuck to predominantly two sets  of eight reps from most of these  exercises and that's something that I've  seen reported in a few places this is  enough to trigger hypertrophy but it  sits at the lower end of that spectrum  presumably that allows them to use  moderately heavy weights however this  might come as a bit of surprise for  those that may be assumed to loosely  with a more for power oriented training  programs and lower rep ranges  interestingly he believed that legs  required more repetitions than upper  body and arms that's not something you  hear a lot these days  though there are more slow twitch muscle  fibers in the legs it's something that  people quote often for the carbs so  certainly it's an interesting concept  many noted the apparent sudden  improvement in Bruce Lee's physique  going into Enter the Dragon